select m.id, 2, $L$Environments & Variables$L$, $L$16 minute read$L$, $L$Variables let one collection run against dev, staging and production without editing a single request. This is the difference between a demo and a real, reusable test suite.$L$, array[$L$Create environments and switch between them$L$, $L$Use variables to remove hardcoded values$L$, $L$Explain variable scopes and their precedence$L$], $L$## Why variables
Hardcoding `https://staging.api.com` into 30 requests means 30 edits when the URL changes. Instead, define `{{baseUrl}}` once and reuse it. Reference any variable in a URL, header or body with double curly braces: `{{baseUrl}}/users/{{userId}}`.

## Environments
An **environment** is a named set of variables — `Dev`, `Staging`, `Prod`. Each defines its own `baseUrl`, `apiKey`, and so on. Switch the environment dropdown and the same collection now hits a different server. No request edits.

## Scopes and precedence
Postman resolves a variable from the narrowest scope outwards:
- **Local** (data/temporary) → **Environment** → **Collection** → **Global**

Set shared defaults at the **collection** scope; put per-environment values (URLs, keys) in the **environment**.

## Reading and writing in scripts
In scripts you read with `pm.environment.get('baseUrl')` and write with `pm.environment.set('token', value)`. Use `pm.collectionVariables` for collection scope. **Secrets** should be set as *secret* variable types so they aren't stored in plain text.$L$, $L$Create a `Dev` environment with `baseUrl = https://reqres.in/api`. Change a request URL from `https://reqres.in/api/users/2` to `{{baseUrl}}/users/{{userId}}`, and set `userId = 2` in the environment. Now duplicate the environment as `Staging` with a different `baseUrl`. Switching the dropdown re-points every request instantly — you edited nothing in the request itself.$L$, $L$- Hardcoding URLs and tokens directly into requests
- Forgetting to select an environment, so `{{baseUrl}}` resolves to nothing
- Confusing scopes — setting a value at collection scope but expecting it per environment$L$, $L$Never paste a real API key or token as a plain variable you might export. Use Postman's *secret* variable type, and keep production credentials out of shared collections entirely.$L$, $L$Create a `Dev` environment with a `baseUrl` variable, then rewrite one of your saved requests to use `{{baseUrl}}` instead of the full URL. Confirm it still returns 200.$L$, $L$If a variable exists at both environment and collection scope, which value wins, and why is that useful?$L$, $L$What syntax references a variable inside a request URL or body? (Answer: double curly braces, e.g. {{baseUrl}})$L$, array[$L$I can create and switch environments$L$, $L$I can replace hardcoded values with variables$L$, $L$I understand variable scope and precedence$L$]
